<Base
  title="Privacy — Remote for OpenCode"
  description="Remote for OpenCode collects nothing. No servers, no analytics, no accounts."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede">Remote for OpenCode collects nothing.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <p class="note">Last updated 3 August 2026.</p>

    <p>
      That is the whole policy, but here is what it means concretely.
    </p>

    <h2>What the app does with your data</h2>
    <p>
      The app connects your iPhone to a Mac you own, running the Go for
      OpenCode companion. Your prompts, your code, your files and the agent's
      replies travel directly between those two devices over a connection
      that is end-to-end encrypted with keys only your devices hold.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No servers of ours.</strong> There is no backend. Nothing of yours passes through a service we run, because there is nothing between your phone and your Mac.</li>
      <li><strong>No analytics, no telemetry, no crash reporting, no advertising.</strong> The app contains no third-party SDKs of any kind.</li>
      <li><strong>No accounts.</strong> Nothing to sign up for, no profile to create.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What leaves your devices</h2>
    <p>Two things, both to services you already control:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Apple's iCloud (your own private database).</strong> Pairing
        exchanges public keys and device names so the two devices can find each
        other. Only public keys, device names and network addresses are stored
        — never your prompts, code or files.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your AI provider, via your Mac.</strong> When the agent runs,
        your Mac's OpenCode installation sends prompts to whichever model
        provider you configured, using your credentials. Those credentials stay
        on your Mac; the phone never sees them.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>On-device processing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Dictation</strong> is transcribed entirely on your iPhone. Audio is never sent to Apple or anyone else.</li>
      <li><strong>Photos and files</strong> you attach are resized on your phone and sent only to your own Mac.</li>
      <li><strong>Face ID / Touch ID</strong> confirms it is you before approving a command. Biometric data never leaves the Secure Enclave.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Notifications</h2>
    <p>
      Push notifications are delivered through Apple's servers via your own
      iCloud account. Payloads deliberately contain no content — no command
      text, no code, no file names. The phone fetches details over the
      encrypted connection to your Mac only after you tap.
    </p>

    <h2>Retention and deletion</h2>
    <p>
      We hold no data, so there is nothing to retain and nothing to request
      deletion of. Everything the app stores lives on your own devices:
      uninstalling and unpairing removes it.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>The app is a developer tool and is not directed at children.</p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions:
      <a href="https://github.com/tjameswilliams/remote-for-opencode/issues">open an issue</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
